# Part 1: Data Preparation & Cleaning

This lab accompanies a pre-registration document. Download it here: [Pre-Registration (PDF)](https://github.com/suyoghc/PSY504_Spring_2026/raw/main/posts/Poisson/Lab_Poisson_Pre-Registration.pdf). Refer to Hypothesis (a) for the variables and analysis plan you'll be implementing.

The data come from the 2010 General Social Survey (GSS). As described in the pre-registration, we are testing whether the number of hours per week a person spends on the Internet (`wwwhr`) is predicted by their vocabulary (`wordsum`), age (`age`), sex (`sex`), religiosity (`reliten`), political orientation (`polviews`), and how often they work from home (`wrkhome`).


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code  code-fold="false"}
data <- read_delim("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jgeller112/psy504-advanced-stats/main/slides/Poisson/data/2010.csv")
```
:::


The following code uses the `naniar` package to clean the data by replacing survey-coded missing values with `NA`.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code  code-fold="false"}
data_pos <- data %>%
  dplyr::select(wwwhr, wordsum, age, sex, reliten, polviews, wrkhome) %>%
  replace_with_na(.,
    replace = list(
      wwwhr = c(-1, 998, 999),
      wordsum = c(-1, 99),
      reliten = c(0, 8, 9),
      polviews = c(0, 8, 9),
      wrkhome = c(0, 8, 9),
      age = c(0, 98, 99)
    ))
```
:::


### Exercise 1.1 {.unnumbered}

Explain what the `replace_with_na` code is doing. Why are values like -1, 998, and 999 being replaced? What would happen to your model if you left these values in the data?

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Hint: GSS coding conventions"}
In many large-scale surveys, numeric codes like -1, 98/99, 998/999 don't represent real values. They're placeholders for "inapplicable," "don't know," or "no answer." Check the GSS codebook if you want to see what each code means for each variable.
:::

### Exercise 1.2 {.unnumbered}

Recode `sex` and `reliten` as factors with meaningful labels. Should `reliten` be treated as ordered or nominal? Justify your choice.

::: {.callout-tip collapse="true" title="Hint: Recoding"}
`sex` is coded 1 = Male, 2 = Female in the GSS. For `reliten`, check the unique values after cleaning. You'll need to decide whether to treat it as an ordered factor or collapse categories. Use `mutate()` with `factor()` or `case_when()`.
:::


::: {.cell}

:::


